The local building community's frustration amplified when media investigations by BusinessDesk and the NZ Herald revealed the extent of Ghee’s wider, insulated asset portfolio. While Sanctum Projects claimed it lacked the cash to pay its tradies in full, separate corporate entities and trusts linked to Ghee and his wife, Joycelyne Ghee (Joycelyne Fei Li Ong), held highly valuable luxury residential assets: [1, 2, 3, 4]
Tuhaere Street Mansion (Ōrākei): Ghee’s interests completed a massive, high-spec luxury home with an eight-car garage and a pool in Ōrākei’s exclusive "dress circle" near Paritai Drive. The property was later put on the market as a "sculptural masterpiece". [1, 2]
Herne Bay Portfolio: Through an entity named Sunlife (NZ), Ghee was linked to an $11.2 million multi-section land parcel across Sarsfield Street and Hamilton Road in Herne Bay (New Zealand’s most expensive suburb), where further luxury home construction was underway. [1]

Court documents and legal representatives for the affected tradespeople have pointed out a historical pattern of shifting assets between various distinct corporate structures, effectively shielding Ghee’s personal wealth and high-end residential holdings from the liabilities of his active construction developments. [1, 2]








































































